a judge in India has heard evidence from  a woman from her hospital bed two years  after she accused the former legislator  of wait coal deep Singh Sangha is  accused of raping the woman in 2017 when  he was 15 he also faces a murder charge  over a car accident that hospitalized  the woman the trial is one of many  sexual assault cases in India that has  caused public outrage the kidnap and  rape of an eight-year-old girl in 2018  drew angry protests figures from 2016  show there were 40,000 rapes registered  an average of about a hundred a day  let's speak now to Indian Supreme Court  lawyer  mahira Sood who joins us via Skype now  from New Delhi thanks very much for  being with us so it just give us your  thoughts then on this particular case  and how it figures into the whole debate  about rape in India and this culture of  seeming culture of impunity there yes  well I think one important thing to  consider is that of all the thousands of  cases that of sexual assault that do  happen in India there there are always  going to be a few that for whatever  reason stand out grab the attention of  the media and grab the attention of the  public so I think we have to keep in  mind firstly that this case is just it's  just one of many and there are many more  stories like this that are not even  being told which should just give you an  idea of how bad the situation really is  in India and how precarious it is for  women and girls here and I think stands  out in this case in particular is the  fact that there is really no protection  for people coming forward to testify  whether they are survivors or witnesses  because what you have in this case is a  political politically powerful person  who has been able to use his power and  influence to compromise the survivors  security arrangements  and to threaten browbeat and actually  assault and in some cases kill the  witnesses and lawyers supporting her  so either through a situation of  incentivizing people to withdraw their  cases or threatening and intimidating  them to to do so what you have is a  situation where cases don't tend to get  prosecuted to their fullest extent and  that's where the political power and the  impunity really comes in because people  know that they can get away with it so  what's the solution then is it enacting  tougher laws or is it is it more than  that is it it doesn't require a whole  changing in attitude and and culture see  I think as far as laws are concerned we  have some very stringent laws on paper  but the problem lies in the their poor  implementation and the fact that you've  not really trained and sensitized the  people responsible for that  implementation whether it is the law  enforcement that is the police or  whether it is the lower judiciary where  the actual trial is conducted that is  the first first sort of point of contact  for a survivor with the legal system and  we have to remember that the people  implementing this at these levels come  from the same society as the people  actually committing these crimes so  beyond the point law is not really the  one-stop solution that it is made out to  be what we need is better social  awareness and sensitization and as far  as laws are concerned we really ought to  be focusing on better witness protection  and protection for survivors so that  they're not afraid to testify good to  speak to you mahira  soon thanks for being with us
 

